---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Created On    : 11-Mar-2009 / alvi
ADN Bugzilla  : -
ADN Change ID : SAPSERVICE073: fixed below mentioned issue with sap import
Cust Bug ID   : -
Platform      : -
Component     : -
Symptoms      : -
Description   : lot's of explanation for one line of code follows :)
		
		Date: Tue, 10 Mar 2009 18:10:17 +0100
		From: Zsolt Gfellner <zsolt.gfellner@adnovum.hu>
		User-Agent: Thunderbird 2.0.0.19 (X11/20081209)
		To: Alvaro Fussen <alvi@adnovum.hu>
		CC: Urs Manser <urs.manser@adnovum.ch>, 
		 Valdas Jasaitis <valdas.jasaitis@adnovum.hu>,
		 pegasus-dev <pegasus-dev@adnovum.ch>
		Subject: Re: status report of sap import
		
		Hi Alvi,
		
		I've checked the following problem again.
		
		>> Valdas Jasaitis wrote:
		>>> hi Urs,
		>>>
		>>> We managed to call all import procedures and now we have two open 
		>>> issues:
		>>> 1. The last records is never imported (status = 0) because it is not 
		>>> triggered for import from the Zsolt's code. When we run once again 
		>>> manually it is imported successfully. We have to look at it afterward.
		>> it is not good to say "Zsolt's code". :-) According to the 
		>> server-side logs (ie. application-log), the calls for the stored 
		>> procedures are ALWAYS executed!
		>> You can give a try with the TestClient for logon-webservices. Check 
		>> for the calls of SapServiceBean.importToLogonDb()!
		>> For example:
		>>
		>>     [exec] 14:29:45,685 DEBUG [      SapServiceBean:122 ]            
		>> SapServiceBean@0x13940bb.importToLogonDb() {
		>>     [exec] Hibernate: CALL 
		>> ulgn01.sapimport_mat.load_tlgns_sap_material()
		>>     [exec] Hibernate: CALL ulgn01.sapimport_bp.load_tlgns_sap_bp()
		>>     [exec] Hibernate: CALL ulgn01.sapimport_cond.load_conditions()
		>>     [exec] Hibernate: CALL 
		>> ulgn01.sapimport_vendor.load_tlgns_sap_vendor()
		>>     [exec] 14:29:45,841 DEBUG [      SapServiceBean:134 ]            
		>> } = (void)
		>>     [exec] 14:29:45,842 DEBUG [      SapServiceBean:110 ]        } = 
		>> (void)
		>>
		>> @Alvi: could you confirm me, please?
		the description of the problem is wrong.
		
		whenever a new business object is processed (i.e. pricing conditions 
		parsed from the soap-request),
		it is persisted in a wrong way.
		
		According to the server-logs the persist and the call for the stored 
		procedures are executed sequentially.
		BUT if we check the logs written by the stored procedures to the 
		TLGNC_LOG at Oracle-side, one can see, that the
		call for the stored procedures are executed, however they don't 
		insert/update any rows!
		
		It means, that the nativeQueries are executed first by the container, 
		and the entities are persisted by the container later.
		(EntityManager.persist() does not flush the changes to the db 
		immediately => this way the stored procedures at oracle-side
		can see nothing new).
		
		this way the actual object (for example: pricing-condition) only saved 
		to the staging area, but not imported to the logon-DB.
		When the second object is processed, the call for the stored procedures 
		processes the previously inserted objects.
		=> this way the last object is never processed. If we've got only one 
		object inserted, it won't be imported to the logon-DB,
		till there is no other object inserted.
		
		I think, it's quite an issue for the SAP-import.
		
		I can see one workaround:
		- calling the stored procedures explicitly from the SapService (i.e.: a 
		standalone, remote method declared on SapService,
		implemented in SapServiceBean).
		
		But you know the persistence in jee5, Alvi. I'm pretty sure, you'll find 
		a better solution.
		If you want me, I can implement my workaround tomorrow.
		
		Cheers,
		zsolt
Fix           : -
Workaround    : -
Release Notes : -
Dependants    : -
Requires      : -
Pkg Changes   : -
Affected Files:

M logon-webservices/etc/gdi/services/templates/SapServiceBean_4all.java.vm

